# Phase 3
# Captured 2026-07-28 on Jetson Orin Nano Dev Kit (JetPack 6 / L4T R36.4.7,
# qemu-system-aarch64 6.2.0, host kernel 5.15.148-tegra).
#
# Command (fixes the networking blocker documented in docs/findings.md,
# 2026-07-28 entry "Orin TCG boot: virtio-net/io-sock failure was really a
# missing virtio-rng device"):
#
#   sudo qemu-system-aarch64 -machine virt,gic-version=3 -accel tcg -cpu max \
#     -smp 2 -m 1G -drive file=disk-qemu,if=none,id=drv0,format=raw \
#     -device virtio-blk-device,drive=drv0 \
#     -netdev user,id=n0 -device virtio-net-device,netdev=n0,mac=52:54:00:11:11:11 \
#     -object rng-random,filename=/dev/urandom,id=rng0 \
#     -device virtio-rng-device,rng=rng0 \
#     -kernel ifs.bin -nographic -serial file:boot-net-final.log \
#     -display none -no-reboot -monitor none
#
# Same output/ifs.bin + output/disk-qemu as orin-tcg-qnx-boot1.log (no IFS
# rebuild was needed — the fix is QEMU command-line only, see findings.md).
# The three -device entries are in the exact order mkqnximage's own
# qemu/runimage template uses for --type=qemu aarch64le builds (disk, net,
# rng) — that order is load-bearing: QEMU assigns fixed virtio-mmio slots
# to -device args in command-line order, and startup.sh's devb-virtio /
# random hardcode smem=0xa003e00 / smem=0xa003a00 for slots 1 and 3.
#
# Honest framing: -netdev user (SLIRP) proves the driver + MMIO wiring
# works end-to-end (real interface, real DHCP lease, real ping RTT) but
# is NOT the br0/tap bridge that docs/orin-port.md step 3 ultimately
# needs for the native-L4T IPC test — that remains a separate, likely
# now-trivial follow-up.

